{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9JrpJt4KarmCppXYNk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016gxBA23XBFtNiKsHPVa1jR","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 90,116p docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md | cut -c1-520; echo; grep -n \"^## \\|^### \" docs/STANDARD-FEATURES.md | head -30; sed -n 1,40p src/emberdeep/TUNING.md | cut -c1-260","description":"Read remainder of matrix notes and tuning doc outline"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14195,"cache_read_input_tokens":169166,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14195},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d255c30e-7719-4824-967e-28ca23bddd23","uuid":"b4fc8373-f0b9-464f-914e-60e7d21ec69e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:53.350Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9JrafvdNTfvcu1j22T","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016gxBA23XBFtNiKsHPVa1jR":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211938__x30f875/work/.research/engine && sed -n 90,116p docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md | cut -c1-520; echo; grep -n \"^## \\|^### \" docs/STANDARD-FEATURES.md | head -30; sed -n 1,40p src/emberdeep/TUNING.md | cut -c1-260","description":"Read remainder of matrix notes and tuning doc outline"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_016gxBA23XBFtNiKsHPVa1jR":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211938__x30f875/work/.research/engine"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016gxBA23XBFtNiKsHPVa1jR","type":"tool_result","content":"- **5.2 Instruments of a game's own** (9 builds extended the synth): `audio.instrument(name, (ctx, bus, time, freq,\n  dur, vol) => ...)`, usable as a music track's `wave`.\n\n## Part 6: menus and the Shell for a TV\n\n- **6.1 `E.Menu` at scale 1 with no scrolling** (TV readability came up in 36 runs; four builds wrote their own menu).\n  `new E.Menu(game, items, { scale, rows })` with the list scrolling under its cursor, the mouse hovering, the rows\n  from `update` (2.1).\n- **6.2 The Shell's 14 px text:** a theme scale for ten feet (`new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale } })`, or `tv: true`:\n  OutBloom's `clamp(18px, 1.6vw, 30px)`); `theme.font` is a family, not a CSS shorthand (two runs' menus came out tiny):\n  warn on a shorthand.\n- **6.3 Settings rows on any Shell screen** (8 builds): a screen of the game's own lists setting keys as rows\n  (`rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY']`), drawn and changed as the settings screen's are.\n- **6.4 The font's characters on the card**, and what it does with any other (`?`), so a game knows before it prints.\n\n## Part 7: the 3D engine\n\n- **7.5 A racer's track** (**prototype**; 7 of 8 WipeoutHD and TrackMania builders wrote their own track physics;\n  ribbon tracks, loops and branches asked 12 times). A ribbon track with loops and any banking, its own colliders, and\n  queries in track space (along, across, up); and a road (`E.Road`) that branches at a junction or runs several\n  tracks (OutBloom, DenshaDeHalo forked road.js for it). Each as a Temporary lab first.\n\n## Order and size\n\nLanded: Part 9 (the suites' own failures) in v0.85.3, Part 0 (the room) in v0.86.1, and in v0.87.0 1.1 to 1.4, 1.10,\nPart 2, 3.5, 3.6, 7.1 to 7.4 and Part 8; in v0.88.0 1.5 to 1.9, 3.1 to 3.3 and 3.7 to 3.11 (3.7's default: F8.9). Then\nParts 4 to 6 (medium); the prototypes 3.4 and 7.5 last, for the owner.\n\n14:## What a game gets, and how it adjusts it\n32:## Requirement matrix\n86:## Action coverage (the presets)\n111:## What stays the game's job\n118:## Verification (v0.23.0)\n# Tuning Emberdeep: Settings' tuning pages\n\nOpen **Settings** from the title or the pause menu (Esc). It is one window for everything the game can change, its pages listed down the side:\n\n- **Game**: Graphics (the view, the camera's zoom and turn, pixel size, frame rate, full screen, lighting, outlines, particles, screen shake, the automatic quality, the overlays), Sound, Interface (damage numbers, health bars, loot labels, the HUD, the tips), \n- **Playtest**: Difficulty (the quick 0.25× to 4× multipliers) and **Tools**. Tools act on the hero you play, and he saves as usual: the whole game is a test bench. You can:\n  - spawn any monster or boss\n  - jump to any depth, the town, the Proving Grounds or the Gallery\n  - grant points and gold, unlock every skill, make items\n  - turn on invulnerability, unlimited resources, no cooldowns, frozen AI, pause and step frame by frame, slow or fast time (the switches last until you reload, and the screen names the ones in force)\n  - record a session for a bug report\n- **Tuning**: the game's rule numbers, a page per part of the game (below).\n- **Help**: every key as bound now, and a \"Where do I change…?\" list; each question jumps straight to the right setting.\n\n- **Hero, Combat, Monsters, Bosses, Loot & gold, Progression, Physics & world, Skills**: one page for each part of the game. Each page:\n  - opens with a short description of what that part does\n  - lists its settings in groups, and each setting says:\n    - what it changes, in plain words\n    - its default and range\n    - the source file that reads it\n  - has a reset for the whole page\n- **Simple / Advanced**: Simple shows the settings that change the game the most, plus any you have changed. Advanced shows every setting.\n- **Find a setting…**: searches every page at once, Graphics to Tuning. It also finds monsters, bosses, items and skills by name, and opens them in their inspector.\n- **Inspectors** (in Monsters, Bosses, Loot and Skills): pick one part and change any number in its definition. For example:\n  - a husk's life\n  - a skeleton's second swing wind-up\n  - a boss's rest between attacks in phase 2\n  - a sword's damage range\n  - an affix's roll range\n  - a skill's cooldown, ember cost or damage\n\n  Each field is explained, and **↺** puts it back.\n- **Copy tuning / Paste tuning** (on every tuning page) move every change you made as a block of JSON. Paste it back later, share it, or give it to Claude and ask to \"make these the new defaults\".\n\nChanges take effect at once and are saved in this browser (`ed:tune` for the settings, `ed:tune:ovr` for inspector edits) until you reset them. While anything differs from the shipped game, a small **TUNED** marker shows at the top of the screen. The Difficult\n\n**When changes apply**\n- **At once:** hero stats, combat rules, gravity and the AI.\n- **From the next spawn:** inspector edits to monster life, damage and size. In Tools, use **Clear enemies** and then **Spawn**, or the inspector's own **Spawn one to test** button.\n- **From the next level:** pack counts per room.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d255c30e-7719-4824-967e-28ca23bddd23","uuid":"f6706f64-3d7f-4c3e-a60c-32a6920031a3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:53.407Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"- **5.2 Instruments of a game's own** (9 builds extended the synth): `audio.instrument(name, (ctx, bus, time, freq,\n  dur, vol) => ...)`, usable as a music track's `wave`.\n\n## Part 6: menus and the Shell for a TV\n\n- **6.1 `E.Menu` at scale 1 with no scrolling** (TV readability came up in 36 runs; four builds wrote their own menu).\n  `new E.Menu(game, items, { scale, rows })` with the list scrolling under its cursor, the mouse hovering, the rows\n  from `update` (2.1).\n- **6.2 The Shell's 14 px text:** a theme scale for ten feet (`new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale } })`, or `tv: true`:\n  OutBloom's `clamp(18px, 1.6vw, 30px)`); `theme.font` is a family, not a CSS shorthand (two runs' menus came out tiny):\n  warn on a shorthand.\n- **6.3 Settings rows on any Shell screen** (8 builds): a screen of the game's own lists setting keys as rows\n  (`rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY']`), drawn and changed as the settings screen's are.\n- **6.4 The font's characters on the card**, and what it does with any other (`?`), so a game knows before it prints.\n\n## Part 7: the 3D engine\n\n- **7.5 A racer's track** (**prototype**; 7 of 8 WipeoutHD and TrackMania builders wrote their own track physics;\n  ribbon tracks, loops and branches asked 12 times). A ribbon track with loops and any banking, its own colliders, and\n  queries in track space (along, across, up); and a road (`E.Road`) that branches at a junction or runs several\n  tracks (OutBloom, DenshaDeHalo forked road.js for it). Each as a Temporary lab first.\n\n## Order and size\n\nLanded: Part 9 (the suites' own failures) in v0.85.3, Part 0 (the room) in v0.86.1, and in v0.87.0 1.1 to 1.4, 1.10,\nPart 2, 3.5, 3.6, 7.1 to 7.4 and Part 8; in v0.88.0 1.5 to 1.9, 3.1 to 3.3 and 3.7 to 3.11 (3.7's default: F8.9). Then\nParts 4 to 6 (medium); the prototypes 3.4 and 7.5 last, for the owner.\n\n14:## What a game gets, and how it adjusts it\n32:## Requirement matrix\n86:## Action coverage (the presets)\n111:## What stays the game's job\n118:## Verification (v0.23.0)\n# Tuning Emberdeep: Settings' tuning pages\n\nOpen **Settings** from the title or the pause menu (Esc). It is one window for everything the game can change, its pages listed down the side:\n\n- **Game**: Graphics (the view, the camera's zoom and turn, pixel size, frame rate, full screen, lighting, outlines, particles, screen shake, the automatic quality, the overlays), Sound, Interface (damage numbers, health bars, loot labels, the HUD, the tips), \n- **Playtest**: Difficulty (the quick 0.25× to 4× multipliers) and **Tools**. Tools act on the hero you play, and he saves as usual: the whole game is a test bench. You can:\n  - spawn any monster or boss\n  - jump to any depth, the town, the Proving Grounds or the Gallery\n  - grant points and gold, unlock every skill, make items\n  - turn on invulnerability, unlimited resources, no cooldowns, frozen AI, pause and step frame by frame, slow or fast time (the switches last until you reload, and the screen names the ones in force)\n  - record a session for a bug report\n- **Tuning**: the game's rule numbers, a page per part of the game (below).\n- **Help**: every key as bound now, and a \"Where do I change…?\" list; each question jumps straight to the right setting.\n\n- **Hero, Combat, Monsters, Bosses, Loot & gold, Progression, Physics & world, Skills**: one page for each part of the game. Each page:\n  - opens with a short description of what that part does\n  - lists its settings in groups, and each setting says:\n    - what it changes, in plain words\n    - its default and range\n    - the source file that reads it\n  - has a reset for the whole page\n- **Simple / Advanced**: Simple shows the settings that change the game the most, plus any you have changed. Advanced shows every setting.\n- **Find a setting…**: searches every page at once, Graphics to Tuning. It also finds monsters, bosses, items and skills by name, and opens them in their inspector.\n- **Inspectors** (in Monsters, Bosses, Loot and Skills): pick one part and change any number in its definition. For example:\n  - a husk's life\n  - a skeleton's second swing wind-up\n  - a boss's rest between attacks in phase 2\n  - a sword's damage range\n  - an affix's roll range\n  - a skill's cooldown, ember cost or damage\n\n  Each field is explained, and **↺** puts it back.\n- **Copy tuning / Paste tuning** (on every tuning page) move every change you made as a block of JSON. Paste it back later, share it, or give it to Claude and ask to \"make these the new defaults\".\n\nChanges take effect at once and are saved in this browser (`ed:tune` for the settings, `ed:tune:ovr` for inspector edits) until you reset them. While anything differs from the shipped game, a small **TUNED** marker shows at the top of the screen. The Difficult\n\n**When changes apply**\n- **At once:** hero stats, combat rules, gravity and the AI.\n- **From the next spawn:** inspector edits to monster life, damage and size. In Tools, use **Clear enemies** and then **Spawn**, or the inspector's own **Spawn one to test** button.\n- **From the next level:** pack counts per room.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016gxBA23XBFtNiKsHPVa1jR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
