.md settles the title and the file names, but Source Information, the feedback questions and the exit interview were left to inference. I treated them as the builder's job and only made sure the plan leaves room for them. Third, how close 'original game' may sit to 'capture its world, characters, themes and tone', when the title must riff on the source's name. I kept the genre, mechanics and tone and invented every name, character and place. Smaller: it isn't said whether the repository's Emberdeep, a Diablo-style game outside the handoff folder, may be drawn on. I assumed yes because it is MIT and the handoff points to it.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Sizing the scope. This genre gets its appeal from breadth (classes, loot, co-op, a long campaign), TASK.md forbids stubs, and other agents will build it without being able to ask me anything. I only learned that builders of quite different capability work from these plans by reading a note in the engine's repository (docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md). So the plan had to work as a full target and still leave a complete game if a builder runs short. That is why it has a fixed build order and an explicit cut order with a never-cut list. It came out at about 16,000 words, which may be more than some builders can use well, and I can't test that from here. The second hardest was the network design: a horde game has to fit the hub's limit of 20 messages a second, and it needed one design that also covers couch co-op and a host with no player, without each skill needing its own netcode.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"1) Say in one sentence what multiplayer 'on its own' must be: couch co-op, online without the hub, or just running normally. 2) Tell the planner who the builders are and roughly what budget they have, so the plan can be sized on purpose rather than by a lucky find in the engine's docs. 3) Give a target length for the plan, or say there is no limit. 4) Mark which sections of TASK.md are for the builder only, or give the planner a shorter version without them. 5) Name Emberdeep in the Tech Stack line as allowed reference material for this kind of game: the kit the engine's playbook names for action RPGs is a small adventure game, and Emberdeep is far closer. 6) Say whether the attract mode's 10-second idle rule also applies while Settings or Credits are open. I excluded those screens, but that was a guess.\"\n  }\n]\nEOF\npython3 -c \"\nimport json\nd = json.load(open('planner-exit-interview.json'))\nqs = ['What did you like most about this prompt?','What was confusing?','What was the hardest thing to do?','What would you change in this prompt?']\nassert [x['question'] for x in d] == qs and all(set(x) == {'question','answer'} and x['answer'] for x in d)\nprint('interview parses,', len(d), 'answers')\n\"\nwc -w PLAN.md; grep -c \"✔\" PLAN.md; grep -n \"GameTitle\\|exit-interview\\|claude-opus-5\\|21:19\\|planner-\" PLAN.md | head","description":"Apply final plan fixes, write and validate exit interview"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01Y7U6tAWzzUAZXSYyr7mR3P":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211938__x30f875/work"}}}
