{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjBvnHhggn8qZmbUmfJR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`PLAN.md` is finished. It covers everything TASK.md asks for, and it follows STAGE.md. The one open point was the file and folder names, and my decision is that the placeholder pattern stays in section 16 and the literal name stays out.\n\n**How both requirements are met:**\n- **STAGE.md:** it says to leave out TASK.md's file and folder names because each builder has their own. A script confirmed that neither the literal name nor this build's model, effort and timestamp tag appears anywhere in the plan.\n- **TASK.md:** it tells each builder what to name the folder and game file, and the plan covers that with the exact pattern plus a pointer to the builder's own TASK.md. When the script filled the pattern's placeholders with this run's values, it reproduced TASK.md's folder name and `.html` name exactly.\n\n**Why the literal name doesn't belong in the plan:** it contains this run's model, effort and timestamp, so it would be wrong for every builder working from the plan. TASK.md requires each builder to use their own name, not the plan to print this one.\n\n**A correction from last time:** I said the pattern bends STAGE.md, and I now think that was wrong. `GameTitle` and `SOURCE` are placeholders in TASK.md too, and the rest of the pattern is placeholders I added, so it contains no actual name. This is my reading of STAGE.md, and the plan now says outright that it holds only the pattern on purpose. The coverage table's rows for the game file and dev folder say the same.\n\n**Files in the folder:**\n- `PLAN.md`: about 18,000 words\n- `planner-exit-interview.json`: all four answers, valid JSON\n\nNothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5593,"cache_read_input_tokens":482357,"cache_creation":{"e